“Do you know the difference between Provisioned Concurrency and Reserved Concurrency in AWS Lambda?”

I replied:
 “Yes—Provisioned Concurrency keeps functions warm to reduce cold starts, while Reserved Concurrency limits the maximum number of concurrent executions to control costs and protect downstream resources.”

We moved on quickly, but later I decided to dig deeper. Here’s what I learned:

AWS Lambda is amazing because it scales automatically. But without proper planning, cold starts can slow down your functions, and unlimited concurrency can overwhelm your systems.

Provisioned Concurrency keeps a pre-initialized pool of Lambda instances ready to handle requests—ideal for latency-sensitive workloads.

 Example: During a Black Friday sale, an e-commerce site anticipates a huge spike in checkout requests. With Provisioned Concurrency, the checkout Lambda is ready instantly—no delays for customers.

Reserved Concurrency puts a ceiling on how many instances of a function can run simultaneously, preventing any single function from monopolizing resources.

 Example: An image-processing Lambda is capped at 50 concurrent executions to ensure it doesn’t overwhelm the database or S3 storage during peak traffic.
